    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thanks so much for this detailed explanation! P.S., I read your CV and saw you graduated from the University of Kansas. I am finishing my dissertation there now! Thanks again for your feedback! </div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div><br><br>On Thursday, October 10, 2019 at 5:33:23 AM UTC-5, Terrence Jorgensen wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Is there a way to turn the entirety of the code that uses these variables into a function so I don&#39;t have to have the analysis code written out 8 times?</div></blockquote><div> </div><div>Sure, you can use mapply(<span style=\"font-family:monospace;background-color:rgb(250,250,250);color:rgb(0,0,0)\">measEq</span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;background-color:rgb(250,250,250);color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;background-color:rgb(250,250,250);color:rgb(0,0,0)\">syntax, </span>parameterization=&quot;theta&quot;, SIMPLIFY = FALSE, ...)</div><div><br></div><div>And feed the varying arguments as vectors of character strings naming the objects you want.  You will just need to convert the strings to names and evaluate them.  Observe carefully how this works:</div><div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">foo </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">7</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>bar </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;foo&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>bar<br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">as</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">name</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">bar</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">eval</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">as</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">name</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">bar</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>So you can use<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> expand.grid() </font>to cross the &quot;factors&quot;, converting strings to names (except for g1, which needs to stay a string).  Here&#39;s a proof of concept you can use to adapt for your case:</div><div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#800\">## create some objects corresponding to your objects</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  assign</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">LETTERS</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># the names of models for different factors</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  assign</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">letters</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># the names of data.frames containing indicators</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>ls</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># see there are objects named a, b, c, A, B, and C. Print them to see they are -1:-3 and 1:3.</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\">## create all combinations</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>foo </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> expand</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">grid</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mft_fact </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> LETTERS</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># store your model-object names</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>                   mft_dat </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> letters</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># store your data.frame names</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>                   g1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> letters</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\">        </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># store your grouping-variable names</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>                   stringsAsFactors </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># make sure they remain characters</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\">## for each column, create a list containing the evaluated names (i.e., models and data.frames)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>mft_fact </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">foo$mft_fact</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">eval</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">as</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">name</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> simplify </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>mft_dat </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">foo$mft_dat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">eval</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">as</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">name</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> simplify </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div><div><br></div><div>When you replace &quot;letters&quot; and such with your actual information, you can print the last 2 lists to see your models and data.frames saved as list elements.  This way might make them look prettier:</div><div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">lapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mft_fact</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> cat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>lapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mft_dat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> head</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div>Finally, you can use<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> mapply() </font>to apply the function iteratively over the lists.  Anything that has only 1 constant value (like &quot;theta&quot;) will be recycled.</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">mapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">measEq</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">syntax</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> parameterization</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;theta&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> SIMPLIFY </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>       configural</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mft_fact</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mft_dat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> foo$g1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><div><br></div></div><div>Of course, you can go even further by defining a custom function that fits several models and compares them using<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> compareFit()</font>, then<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> mapply() </font>that function to do everything at once.  Hopefully nothing breaks (e.g., if a single model doesn&#39;t converge).</div><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792422883149000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2YSsLVDMtOhEIruN9is3sF\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div><div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
